      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to run this macro for my monthly reports but it keeps crashing. i keep getting \"run-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\". can u help me see what's wrong?\n\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Reconciliation\")\n    \n    Dim lastRow As Long\n    lastRow = ws.Cells(ws.Rows.Count, \"A\").End(xlUp).Row\n    \n    For i = 2 To lastRow\n        If ws.Cells(i, 1).Value <> \"\" Then\n            ws.Range(\"B\" & i).Value = ws.Cells(i, 1).Value * 1.05\n        End If\n    Next i\n    \n    ws.Range(\"B1\").Select\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A \"Run-time error '1004'\" in your macro, especially when dealing with worksheets, often means you're trying to perform an action on an object that isn't correctly defined or accessible. It's a common snag, and it's usually not the logic itself, but rather how the code is interacting with the spreadsheet environment. You might need to check if there are any hidden formatting issues or if the sheet name \"Reconciliation\" is spelled exactly right, case-insensitively, but precisely as it appears. Sometimes, even a stray character in the sheet name can cause this error, which is the most likely culprit here.\n\nIf the sheet name is correct, I'd suggest adding `Application.ScreenUpdating = False` at the very beginning of your `Sub` and setting it back to `True` at the end. This can sometimes stop the macro from getting confused while it's running, which is a good practice to prevent unexpected crashes. It's a quick fix that often smooths out these kinds of hiccups.\n\n```vba\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    On Error GoTo ErrorHandler ' Added error handling just in case",
